{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>A widow and a divorcée.</b> It says <b>widow</b> — what does it need to say <b>and a divorcée</b>? I would say: just as a widow, who is permitted to a regular priest, is forbidden to a High Priest — a divorcée, who is forbidden to a regular priest, is it not logical that she should be forbidden to a High Priest? But if so, I would say: a widow who is permitted to a regular priest — the seed of a High Priest from her is ordinary; but a divorcée who is forbidden to a regular priest — the seed of a High Priest from her is a mamzer. Therefore it says גרושה ולא יחלל — he makes them profaned, he does not make mamzers. Or: if it said divorcée and did not say widow, I would say: a divorcée who is forbidden to a regular priest — the seed of a High Priest from her is profaned; but a widow who is permitted to a regular priest — the seed of a High Priest from her is fit. Therefore it says אלמנה ולא יחלל, גרושה ולא יחלל — widow, to be stringent regarding her; divorcée, to be lenient regarding her." "<b>וחללה זנה</b> — missing a vav, like שמש, ירח, עמד, זבולה. וכפר אדמתו עמו." "<b>These he shall not take.</b> By explicit pronouncement." "<b>But only a virgin.</b> — this implies a non-virgin [is prohibited] by a positive commandment and a negative commandment derived from a positive commandment. And if you ask: how is this different from כי אם צלי אש, which is a negative commandment, as we say: he who eats [a Paschal lamb roasted by any other method] receives two lashes — one for נא and one for צלי אש — whereas here, כי אם בתולה is a positive commandment? One may answer: כי אם בתולה stands in relation to יקח אשה, which follows it — and that is a positive commandment. But כי אם צלי אש stands in relation to the negative commandment above it — for there is no positive commandment after it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "כ״ה = כהן הדיוט (regular priest); כ״ג = כהן גדול (High Priest). In the first a-fortiori argument the phrase 'ואינו דין' introduces the inference but the conclusion is then rejected via ת״ל. The logic is: widow (permitted to regular priest) is forbidden to High Priest; divorcée (forbidden to regular priest, i.e. more severe) should a-fortiori be forbidden — but the conclusion drawn is about offspring status, leading to the corrective verse.", 1 "The comment lists other words with defective vav spellings. The last phrase 'וכפר אדמתו עמו' (Lev 25:24, written defectively without vav) is cited as a further example of the same phenomenon.", 3 "Chizkuni distinguishes two instances of כי אם: in our verse it attaches to the positive command 'יקח אשה' that follows, yielding a positive commandment; in the Paschal verse (Exod 12:9) it attaches to the preceding negative command 'אל תאכלו ממנו נא', yielding a negative commandment. The phrase 'אבתריה קאי' means it looks forward to what follows it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:14:14.256575212Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}